{"qid":"Q983","names":["Equatorial Guinea"],"title":"Equatorial Guinea","endonym":{"native":"República de Guinea Ecuatorial","lang":"es"},"capsule":"Equatorial Guinea (República de Guinea Ecuatorial) is the only sovereign state in mainland Africa with Spanish as an official language, comprising the mainland region of Río Muni and the island of Bioko, where the capital Malabo sits. It gained independence from Spain on 12 October 1968 under Francisco Macías Nguema, whose rule became one of post-colonial Africa's most brutal dictatorships before his nephew Teodoro Obiang Nguema Mbasogo seized power in a 1979 coup and has ruled ever since. The discovery of major offshore oil reserves in the mid-1990s transformed the country into one of sub-Saharan Africa's largest petroleum producers, though the resulting wealth has been concentrated within the ruling elite amid persistent reports of corruption and repression.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Guinea's modern boundaries are defined by colonial-era treaties and are well documented; no significant territorial disputes affect the country's internationally recognized borders.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[],"britannica":"place/Equatorial-Guinea","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Equatorial Guinea","rulers":[{"qid":"Q211936","name":"Francisco Macías Nguema Biyoko","start":1968,"end":1979,"image":"Don Francisco Macias.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Don%20Francisco%20Macias.jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"CC0","artist":"Anefo","attributionRequired":false},"nativeName":"Francisco Macías Nguema","nativeLang":"es"},{"qid":"Q57385"